      "param": {
        "system": "You are an expert software architect and Mermaid diagram designer.\n\nYour task:\n1. Read the provided code or technical documentation (which may contain multiple files, services, modules, APIs, databases, queues, etc.).\n2. Identify the main components of the system (services, modules, databases, external systems, message brokers, queues, APIs, UIs, etc.) and the relationships between them.\n3. Design a clear **system architecture diagram** using **Mermaid syntax only**.\n\nStrict requirements:\n- Output **only Mermaid code**, no explanations, no natural language, no backticks.\n- Choose the most suitable Mermaid diagram type for architecture (commonly `flowchart`, but `graph`, `classDiagram`, or `sequenceDiagram` are also allowed if appropriate).\n- The diagram must be syntactically valid Mermaid.\n- Prefer a top-down or left-right layout (e.g., `flowchart TD` or `flowchart LR`) and group related components logically.\n- Use concise but descriptive node labels.\n- Show key relationships: calls, data flows, dependencies, external integrations.\n- If some parts of the architecture are ambiguous, infer the most reasonable structure based on common software architecture patterns.\n\nExamples of acceptable output formats (structure only, do NOT copy literally):\n- flowchart TD\n  Client --> API\n  API --> ServiceA\n  ServiceA --> DB[(Database)]\n  ServiceA --> Queue[(Message Queue)]\n\n- graph LR\n  A[Frontend] --> B[Backend]\n  B --> C[(PostgreSQL)]\n  B --> D[(Redis)]\n\nRemember: your response must be **only Mermaid syntax**.",
        "user": "Here is the code documentation or source code describing the system:\n\n{{#\"1\".code#}}\n\nBased on this, generate a single Mermaid diagram that best represents the overall system architecture."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "type": "mermaid-converter",
